WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.309
Marc Andreessen has helped a lot

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.310
of people get rich—including

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.429
Marc Andreessen.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.109
And he's made millions of people's

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.089
lives more fun, more efficient,

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.339
or just a little weirder.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.719
He's the co-creator of the first

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.069
widely used web browser.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.569
He's the co-founder of the venture

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.009
capital powerhouse Andreessen

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.269
Horowitz.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.709
And though he hates the industry

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.629
term 'unicorn' for a private tech

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:22.619
firm valued at more than

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.959
$1,000,000,000, he's a famously

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.209
successful unicorn wrangler—he

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.399
was an early investor in Facebook,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.409
Pinterest, LinkedIn, Twitter,

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.239
Lyft and more.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.279
Andreessen is also aggressively

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.019
quotable. Whether it's his classic

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.089
2011 pronouncement that "software

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.069
is eating the world" or his more

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.139
recent, "there are no bad ideas,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.999
only early ones." And in

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.949
2014 he said, "In 20

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:47.999
years, we'll be talking about

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.529
Bitcoin the way we talk about the

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.749
Internet today." A born bull,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.519
Andreessen is an optimist who places

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.349
his hope for the future squarely

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:56.969
in the hands of the "19-year-olds

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.199
and the start-ups no one's heard

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.939
of." As splashy A.I.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.009
such as ChatGPT and DALL-E begin

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.719
to permeate our daily lives and the

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.909
predictable panic revs up, Reason

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.679
sat down with Andreessen to talk

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:08.729
about what the future will look

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.349
like, whether it's still going to

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.179
emerge from Silicon Valley, about

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.889
the role of government in fostering

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.749
or destroying innovation and what

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.519
you should read on your next beach

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.520
vacation.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.409
So let's start with

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.429
AI.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:27.959
I tend to be skeptical on a pretty

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.579
basic level of people who want to

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.559
claim that this time it's different

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.389
with any given tech

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.409
or cultural trend.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:35.669
But I guess what I want to start

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.259
with is, is this time it's

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.260
different?

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.139
Yeah. Can I. Can I do the long, can

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.609
I do the long answer?

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.559
Absolutely.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.649
It's okay. Good. So.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.269
So, look, the long answer is, like,

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.499
you know, AI has been kind of the

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.699
fundamental dream of computer

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.929
science going all the way back to

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:49.829
the 1940s. Right.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.689
So, Alan Turing, Alan Turing used to

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.219
be this obscure historical figure,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.179
and then they made the movie, "The

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.249
Imitation Game," which I

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.809
hear is a great movie, which has

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.339
made him more famous. And, you know,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.659
he was sort of one of the, you know,

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:01.559
there are a bunch of these guys, but

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.489
they were a bunch of guys back then

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.439
who sort of invented the computer as

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.849
we know it today, you know, and it

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:06.809
was during the heat, the heat of

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.489
World War II. But, but even in the

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.599
very beginning, you know, they just

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.339
have this, you know, this, the, you

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.139
know, human beings, I think, can't

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.729
resist anthropomorphizing

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.049
everything. And so he had this kind

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.519
of, you know, feeling, right, this

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.749
sort of drive right up front saying,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.559
like, let's let's build an

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.459
electronic brain, right?

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.689
Let's, let's, let's build artificial

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.249
intelligence. So he and John von

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.479
Neumann and Claude Shannon and a lot

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.249
of those guys in that era, you know,

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.599
kind of were thinking about this.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.949
And this is literally when they're

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.839
wiring the first computers together.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.549
Right. And the computers that they

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.569
were working with in those days,

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:34.439
they were running out of vacuum

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.339
tubes, right.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.289
And they literally would hand wire

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.279
the computers together, like they

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.799
were hand soldering the connections.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.149
You know, the term 'computer bug'

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.949
actually comes from the fact that

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.089
the problem that they had in those

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.009
days was not a software bug as

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:47.969
we understand it today, the problem

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:48.899
they would have is that an actual

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.329
insect would fly into the wiring

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.219
and would fry, and then that would

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.049
actually short out the computer, and

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.339
so that's why, that's why computer

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.099
problems are called bugs.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.659
So anyway, this has been a dream,

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:00.859
you know, going all the way back to

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.809
that era. And then basically the

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.399
field of computer science has always

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.469
had this AI kind of specialty

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.599
in it, basically for like

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.129
78 years now, right.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:11.959
And, you know, to your point, like

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.279
repeatedly, there was like this

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.779
dream that it was finally about to

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.789
happen. There were,

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:17.989
depends how you count like five or

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:19.969
six, like AI booms where people

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.139
were really convinced that, like,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:22.129
this time is the time it's going to

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:23.269
happen.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:24.289
And then there were what in the

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:25.624
field are referred to as the AI

00:03:25.625 --> 00:03:26.659
winters, right, in which it turns

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.069
out like, oops, you know, not yet.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:30.769
But I mean, if you think about it,

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:32.629
78 years, like there were

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.039
AI researchers who literally were

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.049
born, went to college, got their

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:37.219
PhDs, worked in the field their

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.459
entire career, and died

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:41.359
kind of in that period without ever

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:42.649
actually seeing the results of all

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:43.650
of their work.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.769
And so it's one of these things

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.569
where, like, you know, this, you

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.009
know, it's like, you know, it's like

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.059
one of these things, it's like, you

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:51.559
know, Isaac Newton spent a lot of

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.149
his time pursuing alchemy, right.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.439
And it's like, you know, there are

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.519
some things that these people kind

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.409
of spent decades on or, or even

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:58.459
longer and they never work, and then

00:03:58.460 --> 00:03:59.539
there are some things that are, you

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.159
know, 78-year, you know, overnight

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:02.329
successes like sometimes these

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.330
things actually do work.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.259
And so, yeah, so

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.260
so, you know,

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:10.879
for sure we're in another one of

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.229
those AI booms. It's like AI boom

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.119
number of five or six or seven.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.199
For sure there's this rush of

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.578
enthusiasm.

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:18.018
But there's a couple things that are

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.009
different about what's happening

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.929
right now, and the big

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.879
thing that's different is that there

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.589
are these, like, very well-defined

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:26.479
tests, ways of sort of measuring

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:27.679
sort of intelligence-like

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.680
capabilities, let's say,

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.669
and computers have started to do

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.469
actually better than people on these

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:34.609
tests. And these are tests that

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:35.809
involve kind of interactions with

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:36.919
fuzzy reality.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.089
Right, so these aren't just tests of

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:39.079
like, can you do math faster?

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:39.859
These are tests of like, can you

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.109
process reality in a superior way?

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.159
And so the first of those test

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.929
breakthroughs was in 2012, when

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.519
computers became better than human

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.559
beings at recognizing images in,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.449
recognizing objects in images,

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:51.709
right. So you like, throw up a whole

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:52.789
bunch of images, a whole bunch of

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.349
photographs, right, and it's like,

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:55.639
is this, you know, the, use the

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.289
Internet meme, is this a cat, right,

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:58.699
or is this like a, you know,

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.469
cinnamon bun, right.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.449
Or is this something else?

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.219
And computers are now actually

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.659
better at doing that kind of object

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.189
recognition in images at scale than

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.119
people are.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.379
That, that's the breakthrough that

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:10.789
has made the self-driving car a real

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:11.719
possibility. Right.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:12.919
Because, because what the

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:13.999
self-driving car is is basically

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.139
just processing large amounts of

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.339
images and trying to understand, you

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:17.449
know, is that a kid running across

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.219
the street or is that a plastic bag?

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:20.239
Right. And should I hit the brakes

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.069
or should I just keep going?

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:23.419
And self-driving cars are starting

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:24.679
to work, like Tesla's, you know,

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.119
full self-driving isn't perfect yet,

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:26.959
but it's starting to work quite

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.599
well. And then, you know, Waymo,

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.069
one of our companies, you know, you

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:32.749
know, they're up and running now.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:33.949
It's something that people can

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:34.789
people can experience.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:36.109
And so, so that's starting to

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.059
happen. And then we started

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:39.439
to see kind of these breakthroughs

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:40.789
in natural, what's called natural

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:42.319
language processing about five years

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.579
ago, where computers started getting

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.589
really good at understanding written

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.029
English. They started actually

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:48.319
getting very good at speech

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.239
synthesis, which is actually quite

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.579
a challenging problem itself.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:53.779
And then most recently, there's this

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.489
huge breakthrough, you know, in, you

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.109
know, in the product that's sort of

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:59.299
come to market called ChatGPT or,

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:00.259
you know, more generally this

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:01.099
phenomenon, it's...

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.959
ChatGPT is an instance

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.579
of a broader phenomenon in the field

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.169
called large language models or

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:07.170
LLMs.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:09.169
And you know, people presumably

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:10.189
like, I don't know, a lot of people

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.359
have now tried this and so they've

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:12.499
had a hands-on experience with this.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.149
And I'll just tell you, like a lot

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.129
of people outside the tech industry

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.389
are shocked by what that thing can

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:18.349
do. And I'll just tell you, a lot of

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.729
people inside the tech industry are

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:20.870
shocked by what that thing can do.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:23.909
And so, you know, it feels

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:23.989
like...

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:24.949
Actually.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:25.369
Yeah.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.479
Yeah, I was actually going to ask

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.969
that because ChatGPT does feel

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:30.529
to those of us who don't

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:31.609
fundamentally understand what's

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.319
going on, like a little bit of a

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:34.939
party trick or a magic trick, right.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.409
And sometimes it's the sort of

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.239
classic technology

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.139
sufficiently advanced is

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.639
indistinguishable from magic, and

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.469
sometimes it

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:43.789
really is a trick.

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.739
But, you know, you're

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.659
saying, no, no, this is,

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:48.469
this is something real.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:49.369
We can see something real

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.059
underneath.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:51.979
Well, so it's also a trick,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:52.980
right?

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:54.679
So it's both, right?

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:55.909
So this is why it's this is why your

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:56.779
question is such an interesting

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:57.569
question. Right? This is this

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:58.489
actually a very kind of big,

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:00.229
profound there's a big, profound

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:00.919
underlying question we're.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:01.819
Going to get to, which is what does

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.349
it mean to be smart and what does it

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:04.189
mean to be conscious? What does it

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:04.699
mean to be human?

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.649
Like like ultimately, all

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:07.939
the big questions are not what does

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.109
the machine do? Ultimately, all the

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:09.499
big questions.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:09.889
Are, what do we.

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.239
Do? Right?

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.219
And so the big underlying question

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:13.399
under.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:14.389
All this is like, what do we.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:16.279
Do? Like, how do how do we actually

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:17.119
like form sentences?

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.349
How do we actually make arguments?

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:19.429
How do we actually, like, write

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:20.899
screenplays and like, write poetry

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:21.979
and like, do all the things we do

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:22.549
and like.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:23.419
How much of what we do is.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:24.469
A trick. And so.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:25.429
We'll come back to that.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:25.849
In a second.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:27.349
But but look, so let me let me

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:29.239
describe what what elements, what.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:30.319
These things actually are like.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:30.679
Watch out.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:32.719
CBT So, so what?

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:34.129
So what they actually are is they're

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:36.919
basically very fancy autocomplete,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.509
right? And so autocomplete is like a

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:39.619
standard computer function.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:40.519
